// F70: `#![deprecated]` on a whole module, which the parser refused until now because "what a
// deprecated module should warn about is still undecided". It is decided: the `use` line, which is
// the one place the dependency is written and so the one line that dropping it means editing.
//
// Both import forms are reported, because both are a `use` of the module: the whole-module form and
// the per-name form. Each `use` is its own site, so importing two names from one deprecated module
// warns twice -- that is the honest answer, since each line has to go.
use oldmod
use oldmod.name
use bare
use bare.twice
use newmod

fn main() {
    print(oldmod.answer())
    print(name)
    print(twice(21))
    print(newmod.answer())
}
